! X resources, merged by .xinitrc before i3 starts.
!
! Avoid apostrophes anywhere in this file, including comments: xrdb runs it
! through cpp, which reads one as an unterminated character literal and warns.
!
! Without an explicit DPI, X derives one from the *combined* virtual screen --
! it divides the total pixel width across every attached output by the total
! physical width, and hands every toolkit that single number. Attaching the 4K
! TV alongside the ultrawide pulls that average down to 67dpi, so Pango sizes
! (the i3 bar and title font, Alacritty) shrink to about 60% of what they are
! on the ultrawide alone, and every $mod+p toggle resizes them again. Pinning
! the value decouples font size from which displays happen to be plugged in.
!
! 109 is the real density of the ultrawide (3440px over 800mm), which is where
! the desktop actually gets used. The TV renders at the same logical density,
! so its UI is physically smaller than it was at 1080p -- X11 has no per-output
! DPI, and that workspace is mostly video, so it is the right trade here.
Xft.dpi: 109

! Cursor size falls back to screen_height_px / 48 when it is unset, so the TV
! going 1080p -> 2160p silently grew the pointer from 30px to 45px. 24 is the
! stock size and matches the native bitmaps in the themes, so it stays sharp.
Xcursor.size: 24
